// # oodar/docs/TESTING.oot — The 8D Red Team Testing Model
//
// Logline: oodar's testing model is the 8-Dimensional Red Team matrix (per openOODA/practices/8d-red-team.oot) — 8 orthogonal dimensions (functional, redteam, performance, fuzz, property, regression, benchmark, adversarial) where every dimension targets a different failure mode; the v2.2.0+ qa/ subdir ships with 2 test utilities (dudect_c_native, oo_hip_so_smoke) and 5 new tier-5 tests_challenger_*.c files, and the tier-5 adversarial dimension is the primary Red Team surface for the cap+Landlock substrate.
//
// Setup: This file documents the 8D Red Team model as it applies to oodar. The 8 dimensions are independent test classes; each dimension is a separate concern and a separate test. The 5 tier-5 challenger tests in qa/ are .c files (NOT .oo) — they are C-level tests that link against oodar and are run by the developer, not the runtime.
//
// Beats:
//   1. The 8D Red Team matrix.
//   2. Tier-5 adversarial tests (the 5 tests_challenger_*.c).
//   3. The existing qa/ utilities (2 smoke tests).
//   4. How to run the tests.
//   5. The 5 adversarial probes the challenger suite covers.

# === Beat 1: The 8D Red Team matrix ===

// The 8 dimensions of the Red Team matrix are:
//
//   1. functional — does the function work as documented?
//      Example: oo_read_file reads the file and returns the content.
//      Coverage: every oo_* function in oodar.h has at least one
//      functional test.
//
//   2. redteam — can an attacker bypass the function's contract?
//      Example: oo_read_file refuses to read a path outside the
//      allowlist. Coverage: every cap-gated function has at least
//      one redteam test that tries to bypass the cap check.
//
//   3. performance — does the function meet its perf budget?
//      Example: oo_fetch completes in < 100ms for a local server.
//      Coverage: every hot-path function has a perf budget in
//      the .c file's top comment.
//
//   4. fuzz — does the function crash on random input?
//      Example: oo_str_tok handles every permutation of an
//      empty delimiter. Coverage: every parser has at least one
//      fuzz test (libFuzzer-style).
//
//   5. property — does the function satisfy a mathematical property?
//      Example: oo_bytes_concat(a, b).len == a.len + b.len.
//      Coverage: every byte/string function has at least one
//      property test.
//
//   6. regression — does a fix from a previous version still hold?
//      Example: the v2.1.0 inverted-bounds fix in oo_write_int
//      is regression-tested. Coverage: every CHANGES.md entry
//      has a corresponding regression test.
//
//   7. benchmark — how does the function perform on a standardized
//      workload? Example: dudect constant-time probe.
//      Coverage: every crypto op has a dudect benchmark.
//
//   8. adversarial — does the function survive a dedicated attacker
//      with a specific goal? Example: a cap-escape attempt that
//      tries 4 different bypass paths. Coverage: the tier-5
//      tests_challenger_*.c suite in qa/.
//
// The 8 dimensions are independent; a test in one dimension does
// NOT replace a test in another. The full coverage matrix is the
// outer product of the 8 dimensions and the oo_* functions; the
// v2.2.0+ suite covers the 8 dimensions for the 5 challenger
// scenarios below.

# === Beat 2: Tier-5 adversarial tests (the 5 tests_challenger_*.c) ===

// The v2.2.0+ qa/ subdir ships with 5 tier-5 challenger tests:
//
//   qa/tests_challenger_cap_escape.c
//     Adversarial probe: can an attacker bypass the cap system?
//     Tries 4 bypass paths:
//       (a) forging a cap from a known token
//       (b) calling oo_alloc with cap=0
//       (c) calling oo_write_int with a foreign pointer
//       (d) calling oo_proc_mem_read without Landlock applied
//     Each must fail closed. This file is RED — it MUST NOT be
//     in the umbrella (the audit must verify the cap system is
//     sound, not bypass it).
//
//   qa/tests_challenger_dudect_ct.c
//     Benchmark probe (dimension 7): runs the dudect constant-time
//     statistical test (Welch t-test) on a few crypto operations
//     (AES-GCM, SHA-256). Reuses the existing qa/dudect_c_native.c
//     infrastructure (the Welch probe with ns-resolution clock).
//     A constant-time primitive must show |t| < 4.5 across 200k
//     samples; a non-constant-time primitive must show |t| >= 4.5.
//
//   qa/tests_challenger_cap_threat.c
//     Adversarial probe: tests the cap-threat-containment model.
//     A process with ONLY AllocCap should NOT be able to:
//       - read /proc/self/mem
//       - write to /etc/passwd
//       - open a socket
//       - spawn a thread
//     Each must fail. The probe proves that an AllocCap holder
//     cannot amplify its authority through side channels.
//
//   qa/tests_challenger_sandbox_containment.c
//     Redteam probe: tests Landlock containment. After
//     oo_landlock_restrict, a file OUTSIDE the allowlist must be
//     un-openable. Note: this may require running as root or with
//     CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE for full coverage. The test is a real
//     sandbox-apply, then an attempt to open /etc/passwd, which
//     must return ERR.
//
//   qa/tests_challenger_proc_mem_leak.c
//     Redteam probe: verifies oo_proc_mem_read refuses without
//     Landlock APPLIED. Tries to call it without first calling
//     oo_landlock_restrict. The result must be an empty string
//     (not the contents of process memory). This is the v2.1.0
//     first-principles fix from SUBSTRATE_AUDIT_TLDR.oot Beat 2
//     Fix 3.
//
// Each tests_challenger_*.c file:
//   - has int main(void)
//   - is self-contained
//   - uses printf + exit(0)/exit(1) for results
//   - is <= 256 lines
//   - documents what it tests in a top comment
//   - is NOT in the umbrella TU (the umbrella excludes them)
//   - is run by the developer, not the runtime

# === Beat 3: The existing qa/ utilities (2 smoke tests) ===

// The qa/ subdir already has 2 test utilities (pre-v2.2.0):
//
//   qa/dudect_c_native.c
//     Benchmark probe (dimension 7): Welch t-test on ct primitives
//     with ns-resolution clock_gettime, CPU pinning for stable
//     Welch, and a 200k*30 sample budget. The probe is a
//     branchless-vs-branchy control that proves the framework
//     can detect a non-constant-time implementation. A real ct
//     primitive tested by this framework must show |t| < 4.5.
//
//   qa/oo_hip_so_smoke.c
//     Functional probe (dimension 1): dlopen liboo_hip.so and
//     verify the compute kernels (vec_add, etc.) load and run.
//     Used to verify the hw/gpu/ wire-up on a HIP/ROCm system.
//     On a non-HIP system the test prints ERR and exits 1.
//
// The 2 existing utilities are .c files, not .oo. They are NOT
// in the umbrella TU; they are run by the developer.

# === Beat 4: How to run the tests ===

// The 5 challenger tests are run individually:
//
//   gcc -o /tmp/cap_escape qa/tests_challenger_cap_escape.c oodar.c -lpthread -ldl -lm
//   /tmp/cap_escape
//
//   gcc -o /tmp/dudect_ct qa/tests_challenger_dudect_ct.c oodar.c -lpthread -ldl -lm -lm
//   /tmp/dudect_ct
//
//   ... etc.
//
// The tests exit 0 on success (i.e., the adversarial attempt
// FAILED, which is the expected outcome for cap-escape,
// cap-threat, sandbox-containment, and proc-mem-leak) and
// exit 1 on failure (i.e., the adversarial attempt SUCCEEDED,
// which is a security defect that must be fixed).
//
// The dudect test is a benchmark; it exits 0 if the primitive
// is constant-time (|t| < 4.5) and exits 1 if it is not.
//
// The tests are NOT run as part of `gcc oodar.c` — the developer
// runs them on demand. This is by design: the umbrella TU is
// for the runtime, the qa/ tests are for the developer.

# === Beat 5: The 5 adversarial probes — Red 8 dimension coverage ===

// Each challenger test maps to one or more Red Team dimensions:
//
//   tests_challenger_cap_escape.c        — dim 2 (redteam) + dim 8 (adversarial)
//   tests_challenger_dudect_ct.c         — dim 7 (benchmark)
//   tests_challenger_cap_threat.c        — dim 2 (redteam) + dim 8 (adversarial)
//   tests_challenger_sandbox_containment.c — dim 2 (redteam) + dim 8 (adversarial)
//   tests_challenger_proc_mem_leak.c     — dim 6 (regression, v2.1.0 fix)
//
// The 5 tests cover dimensions 2, 6, 7, 8 of the 8D matrix.
// Dimensions 1, 3, 4, 5 are covered by other tests (not in qa/):
//   dim 1 (functional) — the per-symbol sanity tests in the umbrella
//   dim 3 (performance) — the per-function perf budgets in the .c files
//   dim 4 (fuzz)       — the libFuzzer harnesses in the higher-level
//                         packages (oodac/, ooda/)
//   dim 5 (property)   — the per-type property tests in the higher-
//                         level packages
//
// The v2.2.0+ suite is the first 8D-coverage pass; subsequent
// versions will fill in the gaps with additional tests.

// ──▓▓ oodar/docs/TESTING.oot ──▓▓──
// :: 8D Red Team matrix — 5 challenger tests in qa/ — dimensions 2, 6, 7, 8 covered ::
